  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 927 AM MDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nThe north wind doth blow this early afternoon as an upper-level \nlow moves across the Rockies. But once the sun nears the horizon, \nthe winds will shift east and increase drastically, for a backdoor\ncold front of formidable strength will reach our area early this\nevening. The backdoor cold front stems from a combination of the \nupper-level low splitting into two this afternoon, with the second\ncirculation moving southwest towards Arizona, and a strong surface\nhigh in the northern Rockies filling the void the upper-level low\nhas left, bringing a surge of cooler air into our region tonight.\n3\nThe cold front boundary shall reach the El Paso/Las Cruces metro \nareas this evening. With this cold front is a tight pressure\ngradient and a low level jet that will bring sustained east-\nnortheast winds of 25 to 35 mph for most of the region. Wind gusts\nexceeding 60 mph are expected for Otero, Hudspeth, and El Paso\nCounties tonight and into tomorrow, particularly along the west\nslopes of the mountains. High Wind Warnings are in effect for \nthese counties, as well as southern Hidalgo and Grant Counties, \nthrough tomorrow night. The rest of the county warning area is \nunder a Wind Advisory, with gusts up to 50 mph, through tomorrow \nnight. Blowing dust is likely with these strong winds in El Paso \nand Hudspeth Counties in Texas, and southern Dona Ana and Luna \nCounties in New Mexico, beginning late this evening. The playas \nin Hidalgo County is also expected to see blowing dust on Friday.\n\nTemperatures for the region will plunge to at least 20 degrees\nbelow normal tonight thanks to the cold air advection associated \nwith the cold front. Friday and Saturday will be a struggle for \ndaytime high temperatures to reach the lower 60s in the lowlands.\nFreezing temperatures are expected for the mountains both on\nFriday and Saturday morning. It is likely we shall not have snow \nor even rain in the mountains, for a surge in moisture in previous\nmodel runs has shifted south. Rain is not expected to be a factor\nfor our region this weekend.\n\nOnce the winds die down Sunday, temperatures will begin their\nsteady climb back to seasonal normals. The cutoff low will be in\nthe Baja by this point and weakened enough to be folded into a\npassing ridge, bringing subtropical moisture into the region as\nearly as Sunday afternoon. The Animas Mountains in the New Mexico\nbootheel will see a slight chance of showers Sunday afternoon, and\na greater chance of rain showers across the region is expected to\nstart Monday afternoon. A new Pacific trough will help facilitate\nshower chances across the region early next week, though how much\nrain the region will get through next Thursday remains to be seen.",
